### When do you choose interface vs abstract class?

The decision is driven by four remaining differences after Java 8.

### What are the four remaining differences?

```java
// Difference 1: CONSTRUCTORS
public abstract class Account {
    protected Account(String accountId, java.math.BigDecimal balance) {
        // Constructor initializes shared state for all subclasses.
    }
}
// Interface: no constructor. Cannot initialize state on creation.

// Difference 2: INSTANCE FIELDS
public abstract class Account {
    protected java.math.BigDecimal balance; // mutable instance field
}
public interface Auditable {
    java.math.BigDecimal MAX = java.math.BigDecimal.TEN; // public static final only
    // BigDecimal balance; -> compile error: no instance fields
}

// Difference 3: ACCESS MODIFIERS
public abstract class Account {
    protected void internalValidate() { } // protected: family only
}
public interface Auditable {
    void generateReport(); // implicitly public
    // protected void internal() { } -> compile error
}

// Difference 4: SINGLE vs MULTIPLE INHERITANCE
class SavingsAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Taxable { }
// class X extends Account, AnotherClass { } -> compile error
```

### What is the decision flowchart?

Step 1: Do you need instance fields (mutable state shared across subclasses)? Yes -> abstract class.

Step 2: Do you need a constructor to initialize state? Yes -> abstract class.

Step 3: Do multiple unrelated classes need this capability? Yes -> interface.

Step 4: Is this a family of related classes (IS-A hierarchy)? Yes -> abstract class.

Step 5: When in doubt -> interface. Use abstract class only when you need constructors or shared mutable state.

### What does this look like in a real banking system?

```java
// Account: abstract class (has state, constructor, template method)
public abstract class Account {
    protected java.math.BigDecimal balance;
    private final String accountId;
    protected Account(String id, java.math.BigDecimal bal) {
        this.accountId = id; this.balance = bal;
    }
    public abstract java.math.BigDecimal calculateInterest();
}

// Auditable: interface (capability, any class can have it)
public interface Auditable {
    java.util.List<String> generateAuditReport();
}

// Taxable: interface (capability, any class can have it)
public interface Taxable {
    java.math.BigDecimal calculateTax();
}

// SavingsAccount: extends abstract class, implements interfaces
public class SavingsAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Taxable {
    // Account: shared state (balance, accountId) + template method
    // Auditable + Taxable: capabilities from interfaces
}
```

### What does Spring use in practice?

```java
// Spring uses interfaces for contracts:
public interface JpaRepository<T, ID> { } // any entity can implement
public interface Serializable { }           // any class can implement

// Spring uses abstract classes for shared state and template methods:
public abstract class AbstractController {
    private WebApplicationContext context; // state: interface cannot have this
    protected abstract ModelAndView handleRequest();
}
```

### How do interface and abstract class compare?

| Aspect | Interface | Abstract Class |
|--------|----------|----------------|
| Instance fields | No (constants only) | Yes |
| Constructors | No | Yes |
| Multiple inheritance | Yes | No |
| Access modifiers | public only | Any |
| Default methods (Java 8) | Yes | N/A (always had bodies) |
| Use case | Capability contract | Family with shared state |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using an abstract class for a stateless contract, wasting the single inheritance slot.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- In Spring Boot, services implement interfaces (loose coupling, multiple implementations). JPA entity base classes are abstract classes (shared @Id, @CreatedDate fields).
- The choice is driven by state: if you need shared mutable state with initialization, abstract class. If you need a contract with optional default behavior, interface.
- Sealed interfaces (Java 17) combine interface flexibility with controlled hierarchy.
